.TH EVENTD 8 "" "Peios" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
eventd \- Peios event, log and metric service
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B eventd
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B eventd
is the single persistent store for everything a Peios system records about
itself, and it answers every query for that data.
.PP
.B Events
are structured records stamped by the kernel. eventd drains them from the
KMES per-CPU ring buffers into sharded SQLite stores, with sequence numbers,
gap detection and a synthetic record written whenever events are lost.
.PP
.B Logs
are text lines, mostly forwarded by the service manager from services'
standard output and standard error. They arrive on a datagram socket.
.PP
.B Metrics
are numeric samples pushed by their producers to a second datagram socket.
eventd collects nothing itself.
.PP
Queries use the PSPU observability query language over a stream socket; see
.BR evctl (1).
.PP
eventd runs under the Peios service manager as a Critical service, after
loregd and authd. It takes no command-line options: all configuration comes
from the registry.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Settings are read from the registry key
.BR Machine\eSystem\eeventd .
The six path values are required and have no compiled defaults:
.TP
.B EventStorePath\fR, \fBLogStorePath\fR, \fBMetricStorePath
Protected directories holding the event shards and metadata database, the
log database and the metric database.
.TP
.B QuerySocketPath\fR, \fBLogSocketPath\fR, \fBMetricSocketPath
The query stream socket and the log and metric datagram sockets.
.PP
Paths apply at the next start. Every tuning value (batching, retention,
query limits, indexing and shedding) has a compiled default and applies
live; an invalid live change is ignored and the last valid value stays in
effect. The standard deployment is offered by
.IR eventd-config.reg ;
installing the package does not apply it. See
.I /usr/share/regman/eventd.regman
for every value, its type, default and when it applies.
.SH SECURITY
Access is enforced on the read path only. Each query is authorized with KACS
AccessCheck against the caller's token, taken from the query socket's peer;
records and fields the caller may not see are filtered out. eventd makes no
access decision of its own. Ingestion continues even when queries cannot be
authorized.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
Begin graceful shutdown.
.TP
.B SIGQUIT
Write a diagnostic dump to standard error, then begin graceful shutdown.
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Re-read configuration from the registry.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B eventd
exits 0 after a graceful shutdown and 1 on a fatal error, which it reports
on standard error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/state/eventd/events/
Event shards and
.IR eventd-meta.db .
.TP
.I /var/state/eventd/logs/logs.db
Log store.
.TP
.I /var/state/eventd/metrics/metrics.db
Metric store.
.TP
.I /run/eventd/query.sock
Query socket.
.TP
.I /run/eventd/log.sock
Log ingestion socket.
.TP
.I /run/eventd/metric.sock
Metric ingestion socket.
.TP
.I /usr/share/regim/eventd-service.reg
Service definition.
.TP
.I /usr/share/regim/eventd-config.reg
Standard configuration, offered but not applied.
.TP
.I /usr/share/regman/eventd.regman
Registry reference.
.PP
These are the standard paths; the registry values above select the actual
ones.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR evctl (1)
.PP
The eventd manual at https://learn.peios.org.
